  "id": "anti-extension",
  "name": "Anti-Extension",
  "definition": "A strict isometric trunk family in which gravity tends to extend the neutral spine while the athlete maintains a straight prone bridge between bilateral forearm and foot contacts. The initial stable-floor fixture has no deliberate spinal, pelvic, hip, knee, shoulder, or elbow repetition: spine.extension is the external tendency being resisted, not a dynamic spine.flexion action. Stable-surface EMG measured greater mean external- and internal-oblique activity than rectus-abdominis activity. The abs-primary and obliques-secondary hierarchy is therefore a practical training-emphasis judgment grounded in the authors' description of rectus abdominis as a prime mover, not an EMG-amplitude ranking. The measured erector-spinae signal is non-mapping context because the visible lowerBack region contains quadratus-lumborum and posterior-serratus surfaces rather than erector-spinae meshes; it is not used to assign lowerBack volume. Serratus, external rotators, triceps, gluteus maximus, vasti, and soleus receive conservative stabilizer credit because the forearm-and-foot bridge materially loads and controls the scapular, shoulder, elbow, hip, knee, ankle, and foot support chain. Those support roles are anatomy-and-mechanics-derived rather than Lehman EMG findings. High-plank, kneeling, unstable, suspended, weighted, long-lever, reaching, shoulder-tap, rowing, dragging, and leg-lift variants remain outside this contract.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "ackland-2008-shoulder-moment-arms",
    "arnold-2010-lower-limb",
    "christophy-2012-lumbar-spine",
    "holzbaur-2005-upper-extremity",
    "seth-2019-shoulder-work",
    "lehman-2005-stable-prone-bridge",
    "cinarli-2025-anti-movement-training"
  ],
